@begin(section)

@title(Recipes)

Short how-tos for common things you can do with zick.  Each recipe
assumes you are inside a zick project (one with a @c(.zick-db)
directory at or above your current directory); run @c(zick init)
first if you do not have one.

@begin(section)
@title(Pinning a Version)

Install a specific version of a package from its archive URL:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
@end(code)

The version is recorded verbatim in the store, and @c(zick verify)
checks the installed files against the recorded checksums — so the
pinned version stays pinned until you upgrade it.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Installing a Package with a Dependency)

Declare that one package depends on another with @c(-u).  The dependee
must already be installed:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
zick add -k myapp -V 0.1.0 -l https://example.com/myapp.zip -u mylib
@end(code)

@c(zick dependers -k mylib) now reports @c(myapp), and @c(zick remove
-k mylib) refuses to remove it unless you pass @c(-c) to cascade.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Upgrading a Package)

Re-add the package with the new version.  zick removes the old files,
puts changed configuration files aside, and keeps unchanged ones:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k mylib -V 1.3.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.3.0.zip
@end(code)

If the new version compares equal to the installed one, zick treats it
as an in-place replacement and errors unless you pass
@c(-i)/@c(--enable-allow-inplace).  Re-adding at a lower version is a
downgrade and errors unless you pass @c(-g)/@c(--enable-allow-downgrades).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Verifying an Install After a Restore)

After a @c(git clean), a restore from backup, or a fresh checkout,
check that the files on disk still match the store:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick verify -k mylib
@end(code)

Exits 0 when everything matches, 3 when the package is not installed,
and 4 when verification finds problems — printing the failing files
grouped by failure kind.  Run @c(zick verify) for every installed
package, or re-add each package to repair the tree.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Recording Metadata Without Downloading)

Record a package from its options alone, without touching the network
or the project tree:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip -W
@end(code)

@c(-W) (download disabled) is useful for recording metadata-only
records, or for tests that exercise the store without network access.
A @c(-W) record has no files; to install them later, re-add the
package with downloading enabled — passing
@c(-i)/@c(--enable-allow-inplace), since re-adding at the already-
recorded version counts as an in-place replacement.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Declaring Configuration Files)

Tell zick which files a package installs are configuration — files a
local edit might change, which upgrades should preserve rather than
clobber.  Pass JSON metadata on the command line:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick add -k myapp -V 0.1.0 -l https://example.com/myapp.zip \
    -m '{"zick": {"config-files": ["etc/myapp.conf"]}}'
@end(code)

zick records such files as @c(config-file) rather than
@c(normal-file): verification only checks that they exist, removal
backs them up (as @c(myapp.0.1.0.backup)) before deleting them, and
upgrades put changed ones aside rather than overwriting your edits.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Removing a Package and Its Dependents)

Remove a package, cascading to the packages that depend on it (sinks
first), with @c(-c):

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k mylib -c
@end(code)

Preview the removal without touching anything:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick remove -k mylib -c -r
@end(code)

The result lists every package that would be removed.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Finding Cruft)

List the installed packages that nothing depends on — a good place to
look when pruning a project:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick orphans
@end(code)

Each reported package is a source node of the dependency graph: nothing
installed depends on it.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Using zick in CI)

A reproducible install step for a checkout in CI looks like this:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
zick init
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
zick add -k myapp -V 0.1.0 -l https://example.com/myapp.zip -u mylib
zick verify -k myapp
@end(code)

Because every @c(add) records the files it installed and every
@c(verify) checks them against the store, the step is repeatable and
auditable: the same commands produce the same tree, and a partial or
corrupt install fails the build with a precise report.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Installing What a Resolver Picked)

When zick is paired with a dependency resolver, the resolver decides
which versions a build needs and returns their URLs; zick installs
exactly those archives:

@begin[lang=sh](code)
# resolver picks versions and prints locations, e.g.:
# mylib 1.2.0 https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
# myapp 0.1.0 https://example.com/myapp.zip

zick init
zick add -k mylib -V 1.2.0 -l https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip
zick add -k myapp -V 0.1.0 -l https://example.com/myapp.zip -u mylib
zick verify -k myapp
@end(code)

zick takes the versions it is given and has no opinion about version
selection, so the two tools compose cleanly in a build.

@end(section)

@end(section)
